# clud-bug-template-version: v1
name: Clud Bug 🐛 Fork Notice

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# SPEC §6.5: "Where a gate's credential cannot reach the change at all — a pull
# request from a fork — the gate MUST NOT block it. The check is set to neutral,
# never passing, and the pull request carries a comment stating that the check
# was skipped and why. […] An unannounced skip is a false green."
#
# The review workflow (clud-bug-review.yml) cannot satisfy either half of that
# rule on a fork pull request, for two mechanical reasons:
#
#   1. "The GITHUB_TOKEN has read-only permissions in pull requests from forked
#      repositories" (GitHub docs, Events that trigger workflows). A `permissions:`
#      block cannot raise it. So that run can post neither a check-run nor the
#      announcement comment — its `gh pr comment` has always silently failed.
#   2. A workflow JOB has no `neutral` conclusion. Job conclusions are success /
#      failure / cancelled / skipped, and GitHub's protected-branch rule is
#      "Required status checks must have a successful, skipped, or neutral status
#      before collaborators can make changes to a protected branch" — so BOTH a
#      green job and a skipped job read as satisfied. There is no job outcome
#      that means "nothing was reviewed".
#
# `pull_request_target` is the one trigger that runs in the base repository's
# context with a writable token, which is exactly what GitHub documents it for:
# workflows that "label or comment on pull requests from forks".
#
# SECURITY — this workflow never touches the fork's code:
#   • no `actions/checkout` at any point, so nothing from the head ref is
#     fetched, built, or executed;
#   • every value that originates with the contributor (head repo name, SHA) is
#     passed through `env:` and read as a shell variable, never interpolated into
#     a `run:` block as `${{ ... }}` where it could close a quote;
#   • permissions are the two this job needs and nothing else;
#   • no repository secret is referenced. `secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN` is the ambient
#     job token, not a stored secret.
# That is the whole job. It posts a neutral check and a comment saying nothing
# was reviewed.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

on:
  pull_request_target:
    types: [opened, reopened, synchronize]

concurrency:
  # Keyed on the PR, not the SHA: a rapid push sequence should leave one notice,
  # and the last run wins because it carries the current head SHA.
  group: clud-bug-fork-notice-${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

# Default to nothing; the single job opts into exactly what it needs.
permissions: {}

jobs:
  # NAME THIS ANYTHING EXCEPT `clud-bug-review`.
  #
  # The merge gate is the check-run named `clud-bug-review` that this job POSTS
  # via the API. If the job were itself named `clud-bug-review`, GitHub would
  # create a second check-run under that name from the job's own conclusion, and
  # the gate would have two producers whose latest-wins race decides the merge.
  # One name, one producer.
  fork-notice:
    # Fork pull requests only. On a same-repo PR the review workflow's own token
    # is writable and it posts the check itself, so this job must stay out of
    # the way — an unconditional run here would be a second producer again.
    if: github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name != github.repository
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      checks: write
      pull-requests: write
    steps:
      # Same primitive as the `review`/`gate` jobs in clud-bug-review.yml
      # (clud-bug#331; SPEC 2.0 §6.3) — this job never checks anything out
      # (see the SECURITY note above the top of this file), so it can't be
      # poisoned by a committed `node_modules/clud-bug` the way a checkout
      # workspace can. It still resolves the CLI by absolute path rather
      # than carrying its own `npx` exception: one invocation shape across
      # every template that calls this CLI, not three plus a footnote.
      - name: Install clud-bug CLI (pinned, isolated)
        run: |
          npm install --prefix "$RUNNER_TEMP/clud-bug" clud-bug@{{CLUD_BUG_VERSION}} \
            --no-package-lock --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund
          echo "CLUD_BUG_BIN=$RUNNER_TEMP/clud-bug/node_modules/.bin/clud-bug" >> "$GITHUB_ENV"

      - name: Post the neutral clud-bug-review check (SPEC §6.5)
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
          BASE_REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
          HEAD_REPO: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name }}
          HEAD_SHA: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}
        run: |
          set -uo pipefail

          # --owner/--repo are explicit because there is no checkout for
          # `gh repo view` to read a remote from.
          "$CLUD_BUG_BIN" post-check-run \
            --sha "$HEAD_SHA" \
            --owner "${BASE_REPO%%/*}" \
            --repo "${BASE_REPO##*/}" \
            --source ci \
            --no-notary \
            --verdict skipped \
            --skip-reason "This pull request was opened from a fork ($HEAD_REPO). GitHub withholds repository secrets from fork-triggered workflows, so the reviewer could not authenticate and no review ran."

      # SPEC 2.0 §4.3 / §2.6 — one comment per pull request, edited in
      # place (clud-bug#259 item 1). Same primitive the review job's three
      # workflow templates ship (`workflow.yml.tmpl` carries the full
      # design notes): one bash function, `upsert_review_comment <pr>
      # <repo> <body>`, that the "Announce the skip" step below sources
      # and calls instead of running its own ad hoc dedup query and a bare
      # `gh pr comment`. That ad hoc query fetched a single page of the
      # OLDEST 100 comments (no `--paginate`) and matched on the comment
      # PREFIX rather than the shared `<!-- written-by: ... -->` marker —
      # on a fork PR that already carries >100 comments before this job
      # ever posts, the query would never find its own prior notice, and
      # every later push would stack a fresh one instead of editing it.
      - name: Write review-comment helper
        run: |
          cat > "$RUNNER_TEMP/upsert-review-comment.sh" <<'UPSERT_HELPER_EOF'
          upsert_review_comment() {
            local pr="$1" repo="$2" body="$3"
            local identity='github-actions[bot]'
            local marker="<!-- written-by: ${identity} -->"
            case "$body" in
              *"$marker"*) ;;
              *) body="${body}"$'\n'"${marker}" ;;
            esac
            # clud-bug#259 item 1 (the residual) — `--paginate` so a busy
            # PR's own prior notice can't fall outside the fetch window. A
            # second residual, same line: a non-zero `gh api` exit (502,
            # rate limit, dropped page) must not be swallowed into the same
            # `existing=""` a genuine "not found" produces — that fell
            # through to CREATE, a second comment. Full design notes in
            # workflow.yml.tmpl.
            local existing="" lookup_status=0
            existing=$(gh api --paginate "repos/${repo}/issues/${pr}/comments?per_page=100" \
              --jq ".[] | select(.user.login == \"${identity}\" and (.body | contains(\"${marker}\"))) | .id" \
              2>/dev/null) || lookup_status=$?
            if [ "$lookup_status" -ne 0 ]; then
              echo "::warning title=Clud Bug 🐛::Could not list existing review comments on PR #${pr} (gh api exited ${lookup_status}) — not posting this round's notice, to avoid stacking a duplicate if one already exists." >&2
              return 1
            fi
            existing=$(printf '%s\n' "$existing" | tail -n1)
            if [ -n "$existing" ]; then
              if gh api --method PATCH "repos/${repo}/issues/comments/${existing}" -f body="$body" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                return 0
              fi
              echo "::warning title=Clud Bug 🐛::Could not edit the prior review comment (id ${existing}); posting a new one instead." >&2
            fi
            # --repo is required here specifically: unlike the review job's
            # copy of this function, this job never runs `actions/checkout`
            # (see the file-level SECURITY note), so there is no local git
            # remote for `gh pr comment` to infer a repo from.
            gh pr comment "$pr" --repo "$repo" --body "$body" || true
          }
          UPSERT_HELPER_EOF

      - name: Announce the skip on the pull request (SPEC §6.5)
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
          BASE_REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
          PR_NUMBER: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
        run: |
          set -uo pipefail

          # shellcheck disable=SC2016  # backticks are literal Markdown code-spans in the comment body, not command substitution — single quotes are correct
          BODY=$(printf '## 🐛 Clud Bug skipped\n\nThis pull request came from a fork, so clud-bug did not review it — **nothing here was checked**.\n\nGitHub deliberately withholds repository secrets from fork-triggered workflows, so the reviewer could not authenticate against Anthropic.\n\nThe `clud-bug-review` check is set to `neutral`, not `success`. Neutral does not block this pull request, and it does not claim the change was reviewed. Review the diff manually before merging.\n')
          source "$RUNNER_TEMP/upsert-review-comment.sh"
          upsert_review_comment "$PR_NUMBER" "$BASE_REPO" "$BODY"
